EFG 2 R "Guarnaschelli et al. (2000), Majority rule" { "Player 1" "Player 2" "Player 3" }
"A Condorcet jury game under majority rule, in the form studied by Feddersen and Pesendorfer: the defendant is convicted if at least two jurors vote to convict. Three jurors each privately observe a signal about the defendant, then vote simultaneously to Convict or Acquit. The defendant is Guilty or Innocent with probability 1/2 each; each juror's signal matches the true state with probability 7/10, independently. Every juror receives 1 if the verdict is correct and -1 if it is wrong. This game was transcribed from Gambit's earlier example file and has not been checked against the paper."

c "" 1 "" { "Guilty" 1/2 "Innocent" 1/2 } 0
c "" 2 "" { "Signal Guilty" 7/10 "Signal Innocent" 3/10 } 0
c "" 3 "" { "Signal Guilty" 7/10 "Signal Innocent" 3/10 } 0
c "" 4 "" { "Signal Guilty" 7/10 "Signal Innocent" 3/10 } 0
p "" 1 1 "" { "Convict" "Acquit" } 0
p "" 2 1 "" { "Convict" "Acquit" } 0
p "" 3 1 "" { "Convict" "Acquit" } 0
t "" 1 "Convict-Guilty" { 1, 1, 1 }
t "" 1 "Convict-Guilty" { 1, 1, 1 }
p "" 3 1 "" { "Convict" "Acquit" } 0
t "" 1 "Convict-Guilty" { 1, 1, 1 }
t "" 2 "Acquit-Guilty" { -1, -1, -1 }
p "" 2 1 "" { "Convict" "Acquit" } 0
p "" 3 1 "" { "Convict" "Acquit" } 0
t "" 1 "Convict-Guilty" { 1, 1, 1 }
t "" 2 "Acquit-Guilty" { -1, -1, -1 }
p "" 3 1 "" { "Convict" "Acquit" } 0
t "" 2 "Acquit-Guilty" { -1, -1, -1 }
t "" 2 "Acquit-Guilty" { -1, -1, -1 }
